# splitux — strip the bogus ID_INPUT_JOYSTICK tag from non-gamepad input devices.
#
# Some keyboards/mice expose extra HID endpoints (System Control, Consumer Control,
# vendor control interfaces) that udev's input_id mis-tags as joysticks. Games with
# an old statically-linked SDL then crash in GetJoystickNames (std::string(NULL) ->
# strlen(NULL) -> SIGABRT) when that phantom joystick coexists with a real pad.
#
# HOW TO USE
#   1. Find your offending device's exact name:
#        for e in /dev/input/event*; do
#          udevadm info -q property -n "$e" | grep -q ID_INPUT_JOYSTICK=1 \
#            && echo "$e $(cat /sys/class/input/$(basename $e)/device/name)"
#        done
#      Anything in that list that is NOT a real game controller is a candidate.
#   2. Copy this file to /etc/udev/rules.d/99-splitux-not-joystick.rules
#   3. Replace the ATTRS{name}== value(s) below with your device name(s). Add one
#      line per device. Match the exact string from step 1.
#   4. sudo udevadm control --reload-rules && sudo udevadm trigger --subsystem-match=input
#
# This removes ONLY the joystick label; the device keeps functioning normally.

# --- examples (edit / duplicate these) -------------------------------------
# ZSA Moonlander keyboard's System Control endpoint (real, confirmed case):
SUBSYSTEM=="input", ENV{ID_INPUT_JOYSTICK}=="1", ATTRS{name}=="ZSA Technology Labs Moonlander Mark I System Control", ENV{ID_INPUT_JOYSTICK}=""

# Template — replace the name with yours:
#SUBSYSTEM=="input", ENV{ID_INPUT_JOYSTICK}=="1", ATTRS{name}=="YOUR DEVICE NAME HERE", ENV{ID_INPUT_JOYSTICK}=""
